A constant list of unit values unrolls.

A loop over a constant iterable is unrolled for a target with no host language,
because a static artifact holds the iterations themselves. That never happened
for a list of unit literals: `parseLiteral` had no case for a unit, so a unit
literal was unevaluable, and anything holding one was unevaluable with it.

The failure was silent and total. A loop over a constant list of durations
rendered **one** element, with its loop variable bound to nothing -- not two,
not an error. Every duration written in a static loop was that.

The list is annotated because a unit literal needs an expected type to resolve
its suffix against; `[20ms, 400ms]` with nothing to infer from is a separate
question, answered elsewhere.

A unit value is a magnitude per base, which is what makes this expressible at
all: `20ms` evaluates to 20, and comes back as a literal in the unit's base. The
two rows below prove both halves -- the count comes from the length and the
`fast`/`slow` from comparing each iteration's own value against `20ms`, so a
wrong binding shows up as the wrong word rather than as nothing.

Only a single-base unit. `measurement` declares five independent bases and its
value is a record with a field per base, so a magnitude cannot be spelled back
in one of them: reducing one anyway dropped the unit on the way out and every
`rem` and `vw` in a style came out a bare number, which
TestUnitArithmetic_MeasurementsAreSpelledAsCSSLengths catches in a browser.
`duration` declares one base, so it reduces.

A consequence, and the model rather than a cost: a written `1s` now folds to
`1000ms`, so Go emits `time.Duration(1000) * time.Millisecond` where it used to
emit `time.Duration(1) * time.Second`. See unit_value_magnitudes.txtar, whose
first line is that a unit value is never its own spelling.

deny out/none/html/index.html `row1=fast` -- the second iteration gets its own value, not the first's
deny out/none/html/index.html `row0=slow` -- likewise the first

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import . "sngl:time"
import app "sngl:app"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
}

const periods list<duration> = [20ms, 400ms]

component main node {
    for var i, p = periods {
        text(value="row{i}=" + (p == 20ms ? "fast" : "slow"))
    }
}

app.window {
    main
}
-- out/none/html/index.html --
<!-- Code generated by sngl (html) from app.sngl; DO NOT EDIT. -->

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html><head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <style>
* { margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }
body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; }
  </style>
</head><body>

<span>row0=fast</span>
<span>row1=slow</span>

<script>
(() => {
  // sngl-entry.js
  var state = {};
})();
</script>
</body></html>
